// heartsim patient case: atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction behind a "sinus tachycardia". Original text by
// the heartsim authors, MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/case.rs.
Case(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "case_flutter_2to1",
    title: "A pulse stuck at 150",
    summary: "A woman short of breath has a regular narrow tachycardia that does not budge. Find what is hiding in the ST segments, unmask it, and decide what the drug did and did not do.",
    patient: "66-year-old retired teacher, two days of breathlessness and a pounding heart, mild high blood pressure, otherwise well, pulse 150 and regular.",
    references: [
        "Brugada J et al. 2019 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with supraventricular tachycardia. Eur Heart J 2020;41:655-720. doi:10.1093/eurheartj/ehz467 (running text on adenosine and macro-re-entrant atrial tachycardia read; tables not read).",
    ],
    stages: [
        Stage(
            title: "Sinus tachycardia?",
            markdown: r#"
**Mrs Papadakis** feels breathless and her heart is pounding. Her pulse is 150 and perfectly regular,
her blood pressure is 124/80 mmHg and she looks comfortable at rest. A colleague says it is probably a
sinus tachycardia from anxiety. You are asked to look again at the strip: the QRS complexes are narrow
and regular, and between them the baseline never quite goes flat.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("flutter_2to1")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "Which finding argues against an ordinary sinus tachycardia?",
                    choices: [
                        "The QRS complexes are narrow",
                        "The rhythm is regular",
                        "The rate stays fixed at exactly 150 however she rests",
                        "She is breathless",
                    ],
                    answer: 2,
                    explanation: "Sinus tachycardia speeds up and slows down with demand and rarely sits at one exact rate. A regular narrow tachycardia that stays near 150 should raise the question of atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction. Narrow and regular fits both.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "In typical flutter the atria beat at about how many beats per minute?",
                    choices: [
                        "About 100",
                        "About 300",
                        "About 600",
                        "About 150",
                    ],
                    answer: 1,
                    explanation: "The flutter circuit turns about 300 times a minute (the usual range is 250 to 350). The AV node cannot follow that, and conducting every second wave gives the ventricular rate of about 150.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "Unmasking the waves",
            markdown: r#"
You warn her that she will feel a brief tightness in the chest. After about eight seconds the
simulator gives **adenosine** as a rapid bolus into a vein (the simulator's dose is the standard one in
its drug table).

For several seconds the ventricles stop. The strip then shows a regular, saw-toothed baseline of
flutter waves with no QRS complexes. After the pause the QRS complexes return.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("flutter_2to1")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(ElapsedMs(8000.0))),
                Sim(Drug(name: "adenosine", dose: 1.0)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What does the strip show during the pause?",
                    choices: [
                        "Sinus rhythm at 70 per minute",
                        "Asystole with a flat line",
                        "Ventricular tachycardia",
                        "Regular flutter waves about 300 per minute that the QRS complexes had been hiding",
                    ],
                    answer: 3,
                    explanation: "Adenosine blocks the AV node for a few seconds. With no QRS complexes to hide them, the flutter waves are plain to see. The atria were never in sinus rhythm.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Did adenosine treat the flutter?",
                    choices: [
                        "Yes: it ended the circuit and restored sinus rhythm",
                        "Yes, but only for the duration of the pause",
                        "It slowed the circuit permanently",
                        "No: it blocked the AV node briefly and revealed the flutter, but the atrial circuit kept turning",
                    ],
                    answer: 3,
                    explanation: "The ESC supraventricular tachycardia guideline text says adenosine does not interrupt macro-re-entrant atrial tachycardias such as flutter. It is a diagnostic aid here: the circuit lies in the atrium and does not need the AV node.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "Fewer waves get through",
            markdown: r#"
A drug that slows the AV node (a calcium-channel blocker, for example) would let fewer flutter waves
through to the ventricles. The simulator takes about two minutes of simulated time to show such a
drug at work, so this page loads a patient whose flutter is conducted **one wave in four** to show
what a slower ventricular response looks like. The atria still beat about 300 times a minute.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("flutter_4to1")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "A drug that slows the AV node is given for atrial flutter. What changes?",
                    choices: [
                        "The flutter circuit stops",
                        "The ventricular rate falls because fewer flutter waves are conducted; the atrial rate is unchanged",
                        "The atrial rate halves",
                        "Nothing, because the AV node is not involved",
                    ],
                    answer: 1,
                    explanation: "Slowing the AV node changes the ratio of atrial waves to QRS complexes, for example from 2:1 to 4:1. The atrium continues to flutter. Slowing the rate relieves symptoms but does not restore sinus rhythm.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "A look-alike",
            markdown: r#"
Another patient's flutter is conducted in a changing ratio, so that the pulse is **irregular**. At a
glance this looks like atrial fibrillation.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("flutter_variable")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "How can flutter with variable block be told from atrial fibrillation?",
                    choices: [
                        "Flutter is always regular",
                        "AF always has a faster rate",
                        "In flutter the waves on the baseline are the same size and shape and keep a fixed spacing; in AF they vary",
                        "Only a blood test can tell them apart",
                    ],
                    answer: 2,
                    explanation: "The ventricular rhythm is irregular in both. The baseline is the clue: flutter waves are uniform and evenly spaced, while the fibrillation waves of AF vary in size, shape and timing.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "Restoring sinus rhythm",
            markdown: r#"
Mrs Papadakis stays short of breath and the team decides to restore sinus rhythm. After sedation, the
simulator loads her rhythm again, waits eight seconds and delivers a **synchronised shock of 100 J**.

A simulated shock can fail: its outcome is drawn from a seeded random generator. In our test run the
first shock ended the flutter and the sinus node took over at about 70 per minute. Look at the strip
to see what happened in yours; the next page starts from sinus rhythm whatever you saw.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("flutter_2to1")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(ElapsedMs(8000.0))),
                Sim(Shock(joules: 100.0, synchronised: true)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "The shock is synchronised. What does the defibrillator wait for before it fires?",
                    choices: [
                        "An R wave, so the shock is not delivered on the T wave",
                        "A flutter wave",
                        "The end of the T wave",
                        "A pause in the rhythm",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "A synchronised shock is timed to the R wave, where the ventricles are least vulnerable. Flutter and supraventricular tachycardias are organised enough to give a clear R wave to sync on.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Why should the team think about stroke prevention around cardioversion of atrial flutter?",
                    choices: [
                        "It makes the shock work better",
                        "Flutter causes high blood pressure",
                        "Because it is a rule for every shock",
                        "Because atria that are not contracting effectively can form clots, so flutter carries a stroke risk like atrial fibrillation",
                    ],
                    answer: 3,
                    explanation: "This is told here and not shown, because the simulator has no clot or anticoagulant. Decisions about anticoagulation are made by the treating team. The simulator does not teach them.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "Afterwards",
            markdown: r#"
The simulator loads **normal sinus rhythm**. She feels much better. Her doctor explains that
typical flutter is a large circuit in the right atrium that runs through a narrow strip of tissue, and
that the rhythm can come back. (A catheter treatment for this exists; the simulator's Template rhythms
cannot show it. Ablation appears only in the EP lab scenarios.)
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "Why can a catheter treatment cure typical flutter?",
                    choices: [
                        "It slows the sinus node",
                        "It breaks the large right-atrial circuit at a narrow strip of tissue that every turn must pass",
                        "It removes the AV node",
                        "It lowers blood pressure",
                    ],
                    answer: 1,
                    explanation: "The common type of flutter is a macro-re-entry around the tricuspid valve that passes through a narrow isthmus. A line of ablation across it breaks the circuit. This is told here, not shown.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
    ],
)
